-- title:
--   Assembling the at-p Néron datum from a Néron object
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N_0 \neq 0$ and a prime $p$ with $p \nmid N_0$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $A$ (the predicate `A.LiesOverPrime p`), a level datum $\Lambda$ of type `LevelData N₀ p A` satisfying `Λ.IsJacobian`, and a Néron object $O$ of type `JZeroNeronObjectAtP N₀ p hpN₀ A hA Λ`. Two monodromy hypotheses are assumed: for every $m$ coprime to $p$, every $\sigma$ in the image `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` of the inertia subgroup of $A$ inside the decomposition subgroup in $\overline{\mathbf{Q}} \simeq_{\mathbf{Q}} \overline{\mathbf{Q}}$, and every $x$ in the $m$-torsion of $J_0(N_0p) =$ `JZero (N₀ * p)`, one has $\sigma \cdot x - x \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}\, m$ (the subgroup generated by the toric points of level $m$); and for every $m > 0$ the same differences lie in $O.\mathrm{finPts}\, m$ (the subgroup generated by the $m$-torsion points whose associated $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}$-point of the object extends to a point over $\operatorname{Spec} A$). The conclusion asserts the existence of a datum $\mathcal{D}$ of type `JZeroNeronAtPDataOrdV22 N₀ p hpN₀ A hA` — a record carrying families of subgroups $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{toric}$, $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{fin}$ indexed by $m$, the invariants $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{toricRank}$, $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{abelianRank}$, the inertia condition `inertia_sub_mem_fin`, and the bound `toric_card_heckeTorsion_le` on the cardinality of the intersection of the toric part with the $\mathfrak{m}$-torsion — such that $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{toric}\, m = O.\mathrm{toricPts}\, m$ and $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{fin}\, m = O.\mathrm{finPts}\, m$ for all $m > 0$, $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{toricRank} = O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and $\mathcal{D}.\mathrm{abelianRank} = 2g_0$, where $g_0$ is `genusFF` of the function field generated over the residue field $k$ of $A$ by $j$ and $j_{N_0}$, namely the $k$-dimension of $H^1(0)$. Two further clauses concern the value at $m = 0$, which the displayed equalities do not constrain: every $x \in \mathcal{D}.\mathrm{toric}\, 0$ is annihilated by both degeneracy pushforwards `degeneracyPushforwardPair N₀ p 0` and `degeneracyPushforwardPair N₀ p 1` into $J_0(N_0)$, and there is $c > 0$ such that every $x \in \mathcal{D}.\mathrm{fin}\, 0$ annihilated by both pushforwards satisfies $c \cdot x \in \mathcal{D}.\mathrm{toric}\, 0$.
--
--   This is the packaging step of the local analysis at $p$ for $J_0(N_0p)$: the geometric information held by a Néron object over the valuation ring $A$ — toric and finite parts of the torsion, their cardinalities, the Frobenius and Hecke relations on the toric part, and the degeneracy maps to level $N_0$ — is transported into the abstract datum used by the level-lowering argument. It is cited in the construction of the at-$p$ datum for $p$-new forms, [`ModularCurve.exists_jZeroNeronAtPDataOrdV22_pNew`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_jZeroNeronAtPDataOrdV22_pNew).
